Exactly. In materials science, a “behavior” is often not stored as an instruction; it is embedded in the structure such that the response emerges automatically when the right load, field, temperature, or chemical condition appears.

A few clean examples:

* Shape-memory alloys: a reversible crystal-phase transformation encodes a preferred shape response. Heat is the trigger; the lattice transformation supplies the behavior.
* Tempered glass: residual compressive stress is literally built into the material, changing how cracks initiate and propagate.
* Composite laminates: fiber orientation encodes directional stiffness. The material “knows” which directions to resist because of its architecture.
* Metamaterials: geometry can dominate composition. Unit-cell shape encodes unusual responses such as negative Poisson behavior, acoustic filtering, or programmed deformation.
* Grain boundaries / precipitates / dislocations: microstructure encodes yield strength, toughness, creep resistance, and fatigue behavior by constraining how deformation can travel.
* Bimetallic strips: differing thermal expansion coefficients plus bonded geometry encode bending in response to temperature.

So the more general representation is:

\text{structure} + \text{boundary conditions} + \text{input}
\rightarrow
\text{constrained state transitions}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}

And that gives you a useful design inversion:

\text{desired behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{required transition/pathway}
\rightarrow
\text{constraints that make that pathway favorable}
\rightarrow
\text{structure that embodies those constraints}

That is a deeper idea than “material properties.” You are effectively compiling behavior into matter.

Once you see it that way, springs, hinges, lattices, gradients, defects, prestress, phase boundaries, anisotropy, and topology all become kinds of physical memory. The structure stores the rule; the environment supplies the query.